# An entry created by a node which had left and rejoined the network never
# reaches a node which joins later: #1 joins nw1, leaves it, rejoins, and creates
# wkayzkkd, and #6 -- which joins nw1 near the end -- ends up without it.
#
# That is both of the shapes this bug has been seen in at once, which is the
# strongest thing known about it. A second failure -- found by a search on this
# tree, where this one was reconstructed from a fail file recorded against
# another branch -- reduced independently to the same two things: an owner which
# had left and rejoined the network before creating the entry, and a node which
# joined after it. Only this one is kept, the other having pinned a subset of it
# at roughly ten times the cost, but two unrelated reductions arriving at the
# same sequence is why both of those facts are worth stating here.
#
# Fixed by moby/moby#53437, which made bulk syncs carry network attachments. This
# failed one in twenty at worst before that landed, and has since run 17,664
# consecutive executions without failing. The rates recorded below are from before
# the fix; they are what the attempts are sized against, so that a regression is
# caught rather than merely likely to be.
#
# moby/moby#53142 left every one of these rates untouched, so the credit is
# #53437's rather than the run of NetworkDB fixes in general.
#
# Reduced by -networkdb.convergence-minimize=optimize from the 16-node, 71-action
# scenario reconstructed from a rapid fail file recorded while validating an
# unrelated pull request. Reducing further is possible and a bad trade: the
# smallest form which still fails is 6 nodes and 12 actions, which fails about 1
# execution in 729 and would cost over two minutes a run against this one's four
# seconds.
#
# The +delays are not padding. Firing every action at one virtual instant overruns
# memberlist's broadcast path, and the cluster then converges only once push/pull
# anti-entropy catches up, some 27s of virtual time later. The cost of that is
# the gossip and probe timers firing across those 27 seconds, not the polling
# that watches for it: at the default convergence step, polling is about a fifth
# of what an execution costs and formation is the largest part. That is measured
# on actions like these: a variant of this scenario with no delays in it waits
# about 27s for anti-entropy, and spreading those same actions 100ms apart brings
# the wait to nothing.
#
# So a scenario with no delays in it is mostly exercising anti-entropy rather than
# gossip, whatever else it is pinning. Spacing one out is not a free win either:
# these bugs turn on actions landing close together. Respacing a variant of this
# one at 50ms thinned its failures by about a factor of three, and at 100ms stopped
# them altogether.
#
# Nine nodes is deliberate. memberlist gives a broadcast a budget of
# RetransmitMult*ceil(log10(N+1)) transmissions spent one per recipient, so N=9
# is the worst budget-to-peers ratio in the range these tests cover, 4 against 8,
# the ceil(log10) step to 8 landing at N=10.
#
# Measured before the fix, three times with -networkdb.convergence-measure, 12
# trials apiece, at about 1 in 8, 1 in 16 and 1 in 20, and 106ms an execution. The
# 92 attempts below are sized from the worst of those rather than the average: a
# budget is only worth stating if it holds on a bad day. At 1 in 20 they give about
# a 99% chance of catching it, for about 9.8s on a run which does not fail. That
# cost is from before the fix too; measured on the fixed tree this scenario runs in
# about 2.8s.
#
# A factor of two between repeats, as above, is ordinary for a 12-trial estimate.
# Re-measure rather than trusting any single figure, this one included.
scenario nodes=9 seed=0x0 attempts=92
         #3:JoinNetwork(nw0)
         #1:JoinNetwork(nw1)
         #8:JoinNetwork(nw1)
         #3:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
         #1:LeaveNetwork(nw1)
         #8:JoinNetwork(nw0)
         #2:JoinNetwork(nw1)
         #4:JoinNetwork(nw0)
         #1:JoinNetwork(nw1)
         #1:CreateEntry(nw1, wkayzkkd=ocbhbqeglhqoetcdbbbb)
         #0:JoinNetwork(nw1)
         #1:JoinNetwork(nw0)
         #5:JoinNetwork(nw0)
  +500ms #1:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
         #8:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
         #5:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
   +10ms #4:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
         #0:JoinNetwork(nw0)
         #1:JoinNetwork(nw0)
         #0:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
  +116ms #1:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
         #1:UpdateEntry(nw1, wkayzkkd=vhrblimc)
         #6:JoinNetwork(nw1)
         #7:JoinNetwork(nw0)
   +13ms #7:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
